                <pb facs="0262" n="162" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg162"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.6363/673: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul at Tampico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Dawson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-05-08T18:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 8, 1920—6
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your May 5, 6 P.M.</p>
                <p>Department takes position that, under the applicable rules and principles of
                    international law, American firms, citizens and corporations may pay customs
                    duties and taxes to persons exercising <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi>
                    authority in Mexico, and, having made such payment, are free from further
                    obligations in matter. Oil operators must decide for themselves as to whether
                    they will pay taxes in advance or not. Standard Oil Company and representatives
                    of other companies have been so advised.</p>
                <p>Department suggests that local representatives of companies should request
                    extension of time limit prescribed, in order that their principals <pb
                        facs="0263" n="163" xml:id="pg163"/> in United States may have ample time to
                    issue necessary instructions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Colby</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
